  55:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** /**
  56:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * @defgroup CO_driver Driver
  57:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * Interface between CAN hardware and CANopenNode.
  58:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
  59:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * @ingroup CO_CANopen_301
  60:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * @{
  61:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * CANopenNode is designed for speed and portability. It runs efficiently on devices from simple 16
  62:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * to PC computers. It can run in multiple threads. Reception of CAN messages is pre-processed with
  63:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * Time critical objects, such as PDO or SYNC are processed in real-time thread and other objects a
  64:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * normal thread. See Flowchart in [README.md](index.html) for more information.
  65:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
  66:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * @anchor CO_obj
  67:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * #### CANopenNode Object
  68:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * CANopenNode is implemented as a collection of different objects, for example SDO, SYNC, Emergenc
  69:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * Heartbeat, etc. Code is written in C language and tries to be object oriented. So each CANopenNo
  70:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * implemented in a pair of .h/.c files. It basically contains a structure with all necessary varia
  71:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * functions which operates on it. CANopenNode Object is usually connected with one or more CAN rec
  72:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * Message Objects. (CAN message Object is a CAN message with specific 11-bit CAN identifier (usual
  73:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * range).)
  74:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
  75:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * #### Hardware interface of CANopenNode
  76:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * It consists of minimum three files:
  77:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * - **CO_driver.h** file declares common functions. This file is part of the CANopenNode. It is in
  78:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *   file from CANopenNode.
  79:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * - **CO_driver_target.h** file declares microcontroller specific type declarations and defines so
  80:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *   necessary for CANopenNode. This file is included from CO_driver.h.
  81:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * - **CO_driver.c** file defines functions declared in CO_driver.h.
  82:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
  83:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * **CO_driver_target.h** and **CO_driver.c** files are specific for each different microcontroller
  84:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * CANopenNode. There are separate projects for different microcontrollers, which usually include C
  85:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * submodule. CANopenNode only includes those two files in the `example` directory and they are bas
  86:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * should be possible to compile the `CANopenNode/example` on any system, however compiled program 
  87:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * CO_driver.h contains documentation for all necessary macros, types and functions.
  88:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
  89:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * See [CANopenNode/Wiki](https://github.com/CANopenNode/CANopenNode/wiki) for a known list of avai
  90:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * of CANopenNode on different systems and microcontrollers. Everybody is welcome to extend the lis
  91:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * own implementation.
  92:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  *
  93:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * Implementation of the hardware interface for specific microcontroller is not always an easy task
  94:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * efficient operation it is necessary to know some parts of the target microcontroller in detail (
  95:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  * (or interrupts), CAN module, etc.).
  96:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h ****  */
  97:CANopen/301/CO_driver.h **** 
